{
  "id": "belgian_popcorn",
  "name": "Popcorn (Belgian slow-soul dance scene)",
  "family": "pop",
  "lineage": "Belgian club and DJ scene that grew from 1969 around De Oude Hoeve in Vrasene, East Flanders, where DJs played slow and mid-tempo American records of the 1950s and 1960s, rhythm and blues, soul, girl-group, Latin-tinged and pop sides, for a distinctive slow dance. DJs and collectors prize obscure 45s with a lilting, unhurried swing, and the repertoire lives on in Flemish clubs and on compilation series such as Popcorn Oldies.",
  "recipe": "classic North American mid-1950s popcorn, functional Song, soul R&B branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. choir doo wop group, saxophone, block chord upright piano, clean strummed single coil electric guitar, upright bass, bronze bell bronze machine hammered symmetric pattern organized rows drum kit. eleventh floor office converted studio 1947-1976 mid tier atlantic studios nyc. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
